```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router7
diagnosis_text:
The failed link is the WAN segment between router7 and router10 (collision domain
link_wan_10_0_0, subnet 10.0.0.0/24). Router7's eth3 interface (10.0.0.1/24) is
administratively or operationally DOWN — confirmed by both the kernel interface
state ("state DOWN") and FRR, which reports "eth3 is down / OSPF not enabled on
this interface." Router10's corresponding eth2 (10.0.0.2/24) remains UP and
healthy, indicating the failure is unilateral and local to router7.

Because the OSPF adjacency between router7 and router10 collapsed, router7 lost
its direct path to router10's LAN (192.168.144.0/24) and to the 10.0.0.0/24
WAN segment. OSPF re-converged and pushed all traffic toward those destinations
over the fallback path through router8 (172.25.1.2, eth5), adding extra hops and
increasing cost from 10 to 30–40. This explains the elevated latency without a
full outage: reachability is preserved through the alternate path, but the
optimal forwarding path is gone.

Root cause: router7's eth3 interface was brought down (likely an `ip link set eth3
down` or equivalent shutdown command), breaking the OSPF peering with router10
on the 10.0.0.1–10.0.0.2 link.

Corrective fix: Bring router7's eth3 back up with `ip link set eth3 up` (and
ensure no `shutdown` is present in the FRR/interface configuration). Once the
interface is UP, OSPF will re-establish the adjacency with router10 and restore
the direct, lower-cost forwarding path.
=== END ===
```